(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"The Clark level diagram maps invasion against skin anatomy. Which layers do levels I through V correspond to?","choices":["I into the papillary dermis; II into the reticular dermis; III into subcutaneous tissue; IV into muscle; V into bone","I confined to the stratum corneum; II into the epidermis; III into the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the dermis-subcutaneous junction","I confined to the epidermis; II into the papillary dermis; III filling the papillary dermis; IV into the reticular dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue","I confined to the epidermis; II into the reticular dermis; III filling the reticular dermis; IV into the papillary dermis; V into the subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Clark level is an ANATOMIC level, unlike Breslow thickness which is a measurement. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does Clark level differ from Breslow thickness, and which does the deck call the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["Clark level is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Breslow is the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is a measured depth; Breslow thickness is the anatomic layer reached, and Clark is the dominant prognostic variable","Both describe the same measurement on different scales, and either may be used as the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Clark is the dominant prognostic variable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Breslow is measured in millimetres and drives margins and staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"In the TNM melanoma staging overview, what do the three letters denote?","choices":["T is primary tumour thickness; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of metastases at a distant site","T is the tumour's anatomic level; N is the number of naevi present; M is the number of mitoses per high-power field","T is the time since onset; N is the number of tumour-involved regional lymph nodes; M is the number of distant metastases","T is primary tumour thickness; N is the nodal diameter in centimetres; M is the mitotic rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the footnote of the staging table defines all three. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which TNM combination defines stage 0 melanoma?","choices":["Tis, N1a, M0","T0, N0, M1","Tis, N0, M0","T1a, N0, M0"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ with no nodal or distant disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What defines stage IV melanoma in the staging overview?","choices":["Any T, any N, with M1 — that is, distant metastasis present whatever the primary or nodal status","T4b, N3a/b/c, with M0 — the deepest primary with extensive nodal disease but no distant spread","Any T with N0 and M0 — a thick primary with no nodal or distant disease","Tis with any N and any M — in situ disease with any degree of spread"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — M1 alone determines it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what distinguishes stage III from stage IV?","choices":["Stage III is melanoma confined to the epidermis; stage IV is localised disease in the skin","Stage III is spread to the lymph nodes; stage IV is spread to other organs","Stage III is spread to other organs; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes","Stage III is localised disease thicker than stage II; stage IV is spread to the lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nodal against distant spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"In the Stages of Melanoma diagram, what are stage 0, stage I and stage II?","choices":["Stage 0 is localised disease only in the skin and very thin; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes","Stage 0 is disease that has spread to the lymph nodes; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is localised disease that is very thin","Stage 0 is localised disease thicker than stage I; stage I is melanoma confined to the epidermal region; stage II is disease that has spread to other organs","Stage 0 is melanoma confined to the epidermal region of the skin; stage I is localised disease, only in skin and very thin; stage II is localised disease thicker than stage I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 0, I and II are all still local. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A single nail abnormality has persisted despite treatment. What does the deck say to avoid?","choices":["Repeated empiric antifungal, antibiotic or wart treatment","Photographing the concerning nail before any referral is made","Examining the other nine nails","Removing the nail polish before examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — repeating empiric therapy on a solitary persistent nail lesion is how diagnostic delay happens. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"The deck says to photograph a concerning pigmented band. What caution comes with that?","choices":["Do not photograph it at all, because nail pigment renders inaccurately on a phone camera","Do not photograph it until the polish has regrown","Do not photograph more than one nail at a time","Do not let photographing it delay referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — documentation is for tracking change, not for postponing the specialist. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What harm does the deck attribute to overly aggressive cutaneous T-cell lymphoma therapy?","choices":["Permanent phototherapy intolerance","Loss of eligibility for a cutaneous lymphoma centre","Complications and premature death","Accelerated transformation into a systemic non-Hodgkin lymphoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why must oedema severity not be used to judge Kaposi sarcoma burden?","choices":["Oedema in Kaposi sarcoma is always caused by the antiretroviral therapy itself","Oedema only appears once visceral disease is established","Oedema resolves within days of starting treatment","Marked oedema may occur with few or no visible skin lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck says explicitly not to use oedema severity alone. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A course of field therapy has cleared a patient's actinic keratoses. What must not be assumed?","choices":["That the treatment reactions of erythema and crusting were abnormal and should not recur","That daily sun protection is still required","That a nonhealing lesion should be reviewed promptly","That surveillance can stop, because the surrounding field remains at risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ongoing surveillance remains necessary despite successful therapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome?","choices":["An allergic reaction to liposomal doxorubicin","Kaposi sarcoma present when antiretroviral therapy begins can worsen as the immune system recovers","Kaposi sarcoma appearing for the first time several years after antiretroviral therapy begins","Kaposi sarcoma that fails to respond to antiretroviral therapy at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the treatment itself can make the disease worse before it improves. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"In which patients is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome especially dangerous?","choices":["Those with classic Kaposi sarcoma, which is indolent","Those with only oral lesions","Those over seventy years old","Those with visceral disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — endemic or visceral disease and this syndrome can be aggressive and rapidly fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Where does the morbidity of basal cell carcinoma come from?","choices":["Transformation into squamous cell carcinoma","Local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites","Nodal metastasis, which occurs in three to seven per cent of all treated cases","Perineural spread to the skull base in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — metastatic disease is rare but serious, and hedgehog inhibitor therapy exists for it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What increases recurrence and functional morbidity in nail unit squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Incomplete excision, immunosuppression, delayed diagnosis and bone invasion","Complete margin-controlled surgery with clear histologic margins","Photographing the lesion before referral","A negative human papillomavirus result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is often curable with complete surgery, which is why incompleteness matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What must a patient starting antiretroviral therapy for Kaposi sarcoma be told?","choices":["That the lesions will always begin improving within the first two weeks of therapy","That antiretroviral therapy should be stopped if lesions enlarge","That oral lesions never respond to therapy","That the lesions may worsen early, as immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — warning them beforehand stops early worsening being read as treatment failure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype does the deck say is frequently delayed in diagnosis, making early reporting critical?","choices":["Acral lentiginous melanoma","Superficial spreading melanoma, the commonest subtype overall","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Nodular melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why patients are told to report new pigment, widening bands or periungual pigment promptly. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What does the deck say patients should be told about establishing the cutaneous T-cell lymphoma diagnosis?","choices":["Blood testing establishes the diagnosis before any biopsy","It may require repeated biopsies, and the disease is usually chronic","A single biopsy is definitive in nearly all cases, and the disease is self-limiting","The diagnosis is made clinically and biopsy is unnecessary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — setting that expectation early matters, because the diagnosis is often slow to confirm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which two things must a patient on topical therapy for basal cell carcinoma understand?","choices":["That clearance is assumed once the lesion is no longer visible","That sun protection is unnecessary once treatment begins","That local inflammation is expected, and that clearance has to be confirmed afterwards","That inflammation means the drug has failed and that treatment should be stopped early"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the inflammation is not a reason to stop, and clearing visibly is not the same as being clear. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which local reactions should an actinic keratosis patient be warned to expect from field therapy?","choices":["Blistering that requires the course to be abandoned","No visible reaction at all if the therapy is working","Erythema and crusting, alongside adherence to the full treatment duration","Permanent hypopigmentation at every treated site, which is expected and harmless"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — patients who are not warned stop the course early. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype list determines behaviour and treatment?","choices":["The CLINICAL list: superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform subtypes","Either list, since the two are identical","Neither, since size determines treatment","The HISTOLOGIC list: superficial, nodular, micronodular and infiltrative"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and note it differs from the CLINICAL list, which is superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What must be assessed when planning basal cell carcinoma treatment?","choices":["Whether the patient can tolerate hedgehog inhibitors","Whether it is low-risk versus morpheaform, micronodular, infiltrative, recurrent, or in a tissue-sensitive site","Whether the patient has previously taken oral nicotinamide as chemoprevention for a prior lesion","Whether the lesion is pigmented or not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck says this directly changes treatment selection. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which lesion features make early squamous cell carcinoma the distinction to exclude in an actinic keratosis?","choices":["Thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent","Rough, flat, flesh-coloured, and more apparent by touch than it is by sight","Pearly, translucent, with telangiectasias","Scaly, symmetric and under two millimetres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck's full list of features that shift the concern to carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Besides sun exposure and immunosuppression, which risk factors does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Red tattoo ink, laser treatment and cryotherapy","Chronic wounds, scars or prior radiation fields; certain genetic diseases; and mucosal or genital disease","Tanning bed use, fair skin that burns easily, male sex, and outdoor occupation without protection","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three additional categories as listed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"In which three immunosuppressed groups is basal cell carcinoma more common and more likely to recur?","choices":["Diabetes, chronic kidney disease and cirrhosis","Pregnancy, and long-term oral contraceptive or corticosteroid use","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma, solid-organ transplant, and allogeneic stem cell transplant","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, human immunodeficiency virus, and organ transplantation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three groups named on the risk factor slide. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are the four possible fates of an individual actinic keratosis?","choices":["It may persist, involute, recur, or progress","It may persist, progress, metastasise to nodes, or recur after treatment","It may only persist or progress","It will always progress if left untreated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — including involution, which is why individual lesion behaviour is unpredictable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which cutaneous T-cell lymphoma features worsen prognosis, and which carries the best?","choices":["Only lymphadenopathy worsens it; skin findings do not affect prognosis","Prognosis is uniformly poor regardless of stage","Tumours, erythroderma and lymphadenopathy worsen it; limited patch disease does not reduce survival","Patch and plaque disease worsen it; tumour-stage disease carries the better prognosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — survival is not reduced in limited patch-stage disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which Kaposi sarcoma form may REGRESS, and what makes it do so?","choices":["Epidemic disease, which regresses once chemotherapy stops","Iatrogenic disease, which may regress when immunosuppression is reduced","Classic disease, which regresses spontaneously after a course of local radiation","Endemic disease, which regresses with antiretroviral therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why the transplant team is consulted before any reduction. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which team must be involved BEFORE immunosuppression is reduced in iatrogenic Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Pulmonology or gastroenterology","The transplant team","Medical oncology, who direct all systemic and immunosuppressive decisions","Palliative care"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck lists them explicitly as a referral before reducing immunosuppression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"}]};
